import Foundation

// MARK: - Scripture Content Expansion (Entries 9–20)

extension ContentLibrary {

    static let scriptureContentExpansion: [DayContent] = [
        // Entry 9
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Psalm 119:130",
            scriptureText: "The unfolding of your words gives light; it gives understanding to the simple.",
            devotionalTitle: "Light That Unfolds",
            devotionalText: "God's Word doesn't always hit you all at once. It unfolds—slowly, gently, like dawn breaking over the horizon. You don't need a seminary degree to understand Scripture; God promises that even the simple gain understanding when His words open up. The key is patient, attentive reading. As you return to the same passages day after day, new layers of meaning emerge. Trust that every time you open the Bible, God is unfolding light for your specific moment.",
            prayerText: "Father, thank You that Your Word unfolds with light and understanding. I don't need to have all the answers before I begin—I just need to open Your book and trust You to illuminate the truth. Where I feel confused, bring clarity. Where I feel simple and inadequate, remind me that You delight in giving understanding to humble hearts. Unfold Your words before me today. Let each verse carry a fresh brightness into the places where I need direction. I receive Your light with gratitude. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there a passage you've read many times that recently revealed something new to you?",
            actionSteps: ["Reread a familiar passage slowly three times, writing down anything new you notice", "Share a fresh insight from Scripture with someone in your life today"]
        ),
        // Entry 10
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Romans 15:4",
            scriptureText: "For everything that was written in the past was written to teach us, so that through the endurance and the encouragement of the Scriptures we might have hope.",
            devotionalTitle: "Written for Your Hope",
            devotionalText: "The stories of Abraham, Moses, David, and Ruth weren't recorded merely as history lessons. Paul tells us they were written for you—to build endurance when you're weary and encouragement when you're downcast. Scripture is a reservoir of hope. Every narrative of God's faithfulness in the past is a promise that He will be faithful in your present. When life feels hopeless, the Bible doesn't offer empty optimism; it offers the track record of a God who has never failed.",
            prayerText: "Lord, thank You that everything written in Your Word was written for me—for my endurance, my encouragement, and my hope. When I am weary, let the stories of Your faithfulness in Scripture renew my strength. When I am discouraged, let the promises You kept for others remind me that You will keep Your promises to me. Fill me with hope today as I read, not a wishful hope, but a confident expectation rooted in who You are. I trust Your track record, Father. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Which story from the Old Testament most encourages you when you feel like giving up?",
            actionSteps: ["Read one Old Testament narrative today and write down what it reveals about God's character", "Encourage a friend by texting them a verse that has given you hope"]
        ),
        // Entry 11
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Deuteronomy 8:3",
            scriptureText: "He humbled you, causing you to hunger and then feeding you with manna, which neither you nor your ancestors had known, to teach you that man does not live on bread alone but on every word that comes from the mouth of the Lord.",
            devotionalTitle: "More Than Bread",
            devotionalText: "In the wilderness, God allowed His people to grow hungry before He fed them with manna. It wasn't cruelty—it was a lesson. Physical food sustains the body, but God's Word sustains the soul. When you feel spiritually empty, it may be because you've been feeding on everything except the Word. The manna was new every morning, and so is God's mercy in Scripture. Come to the Bible today not as a task to complete, but as a table that has been set for you.",
            prayerText: "God, I confess that I sometimes try to live on bread alone—filling my life with distractions while my soul goes hungry. Teach me, as You taught Israel in the wilderness, that I need every word that comes from Your mouth. Humble me enough to admit my need. Feed me with the manna of Your truth, fresh and sufficient for today. I come to Your table hungry, Lord. Satisfy me with what the world cannot offer. Restore my soul through the nourishment of Your Word. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What have you been feeding on that leaves your soul hungry?",
            actionSteps: ["Before breakfast tomorrow, spend ten minutes reading Scripture—feed your spirit before your body", "Identify one distraction you can reduce this week to make more room for God's Word"]
        ),
        // Entry 12
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Proverbs 30:5",
            scriptureText: "Every word of God is flawless; he is a shield to those who take refuge in him.",
            devotionalTitle: "Flawless and Faithful",
            devotionalText: "In a world saturated with half-truths, shifting opinions, and unreliable promises, God's Word stands apart: every word is flawless. Not most words. Every word. This verse pairs that truth with a beautiful image—God is a shield to those who take refuge in Him. When you trust Scripture, you aren't just accepting information; you are stepping behind the shield of the Almighty. His Word is both trustworthy and protective. You can stake your life on it without fear.",
            prayerText: "Lord, in a world full of confusion and competing voices, I anchor myself to this truth: every word of Yours is flawless. I don't have to doubt, second-guess, or look elsewhere for solid ground. You are my refuge and my shield. Protect me today from deception by grounding me deeply in Your perfect Word. When I face uncertainty, let Scripture be the voice I trust above all others. I choose to take refuge in You and in the flawless truth You have spoken. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there a promise in Scripture you struggle to fully trust? What would it look like to take refuge in it?",
            actionSteps: ["Write down one promise from Scripture and carry it with you as a reminder of God's reliability", "When you encounter conflicting advice today, pause and ask what Scripture says about it"]
        ),
        // Entry 13
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Colossians 3:16",
            scriptureText: "Let the message of Christ dwell among you richly as you teach and admonish one another with all wisdom through psalms, hymns, and songs from the Spirit, singing to God with gratitude in your hearts.",
            devotionalTitle: "Richly Dwelling",
            devotionalText: "Paul envisions Scripture not as something we visit occasionally, but as something that dwells richly within us and among us. Notice the communal dimension: we teach and admonish one another through the Word. Bible engagement was never meant to be a solitary discipline alone. When God's Word fills a community, it overflows in wisdom, worship, and mutual encouragement. Let the message of Christ take up residence in your heart—not as a guest, but as the owner of the house.",
            prayerText: "Lord Jesus, let Your message dwell in me richly—not skimming the surface, but soaking into the deepest parts of who I am. Give me wisdom to teach and encourage others with what I am learning. Fill my heart with gratitude that spills over into songs and praise. I don't want Your Word to be a visitor in my life; I want it to be at home in me. Draw me into community where we can open the Bible together and be shaped by Your truth. In Your name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How could you share what you're learning in Scripture with someone in your community this week?",
            actionSteps: ["Invite a friend to read the same passage this week and discuss it together", "Listen to a worship song based on Scripture and meditate on the lyrics"]
        ),
        // Entry 14
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "John 8:31-32",
            scriptureText: "To the Jews who had believed him, Jesus said, 'If you hold to my teaching, you are really my disciples. Then you will know the truth, and the truth will set you free.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Truth That Sets You Free",
            devotionalText: "Freedom is one of the deepest longings of the human heart, and Jesus says the path to it runs directly through His Word. But notice the condition: holding to His teaching. This isn't a casual glance at Scripture—it's a committed, ongoing grip. The truth that sets you free isn't a single inspiring verse; it's a life immersed in God's Word over time. Every lie that holds you captive—about your identity, your worth, your future—loses its power when confronted with the truth of Jesus.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, I want to be set free by Your truth. Show me the lies I've believed about myself, about others, and about You. As I hold to Your teaching, let truth replace every deception. I commit to staying in Your Word—not just visiting it—because that is where true freedom is found. Break every chain that the enemy has used to hold me back. I am Your disciple, and I choose to live in the freedom You have purchased for me. In Your name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What is one lie you have believed that God's Word contradicts?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify a lie you struggle with and find a Scripture that speaks the opposite truth—write both down", "Share your experience of freedom in Christ with someone who may be feeling trapped"]
        ),
        // Entry 15
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Psalm 19:7-8",
            scriptureText: "The law of the Lord is perfect, refreshing the soul. The statutes of the Lord are trustworthy, making wise the simple. The precepts of the Lord are right, giving joy to the heart. The commands of the Lord are radiant, giving light to the eyes.",
            devotionalTitle: "Perfect, Trustworthy, Right, Radiant",
            devotionalText: "David stacks up four descriptions of God's Word, each paired with a gift it brings. Perfect—it refreshes your weary soul. Trustworthy—it makes the uncertain wise. Right—it fills the heavy heart with joy. Radiant—it illuminates what was hidden in darkness. This is not a dry legal code; this is living water, proven counsel, deep gladness, and brilliant clarity. Whatever you need today, Scripture has something to offer. Come to it expectantly.",
            prayerText: "Lord, Your Word is perfect, and my soul needs refreshing. Your statutes are trustworthy, and I need wisdom for the decisions ahead. Your precepts are right, and my heart longs for true joy. Your commands are radiant, and I need light in the darkness around me. I come to Scripture today not out of habit, but out of need. Meet me in Your Word. Refresh, instruct, gladden, and illuminate me by the power of Your truth. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Which of these four gifts—refreshment, wisdom, joy, or light—do you need most today?",
            actionSteps: ["Read Psalm 19 in its entirety and journal about which verse speaks most directly to your current season", "Choose one attribute of God's Word from this passage and thank Him for it throughout the day"]
        ),
        // Entry 16
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Romans 10:17",
            scriptureText: "Consequently, faith comes from hearing the message, and the message is heard through the word about Christ.",
            devotionalTitle: "Faith That Comes by Hearing",
            devotionalText: "If you want stronger faith, Paul gives you the prescription: hear the Word of Christ. Faith is not manufactured by willpower or positive thinking; it is grown through exposure to Scripture. Every time you read, listen to, or meditate on God's Word, your capacity to trust Him expands. This is why consistent Bible engagement matters—not as a religious requirement, but as the very mechanism by which faith takes root and grows in your life.",
            prayerText: "Father, I want my faith to grow deeper and stronger. I know that faith comes from hearing Your Word, so draw me into Scripture with fresh hunger. When doubt creeps in, let me run to the Bible rather than away from it. Increase my faith as I hear the message about Christ—His life, His death, His resurrection, and His promises. I choose to build my faith on what You have said, not on what I feel. Let Your Word be the soil in which my faith flourishes. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How has regular time in Scripture affected the strength of your faith over time?",
            actionSteps: ["Listen to an audio reading of a Gospel chapter today, paying attention to what it reveals about Jesus", "Write down one area where your faith feels weak and find a Scripture that speaks directly to it"]
        ),
        // Entry 17
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Acts 17:11",
            scriptureText: "Now the Berean Jews were of more noble character than those in Thessalonica, for they received the message with great eagerness and examined the Scriptures every day to see if what Paul said was true.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Berean Standard",
            devotionalText: "The Bereans are commended for two qualities: eagerness and discernment. They didn't reject Paul's message, nor did they accept it uncritically. They received it with great eagerness and then examined the Scriptures daily to verify it. This is the model for every believer. Be hungry for teaching, but always test it against God's Word. A mature Christian is not one who blindly follows any teacher, but one who knows the Bible well enough to recognize truth from error.",
            prayerText: "Lord, give me the spirit of the Bereans—eager to learn and diligent to examine everything against Your Word. Protect me from both cynicism and gullibility. Help me to receive good teaching with open arms while keeping Scripture as my ultimate authority. Make me a student of Your Word who knows it deeply enough to discern truth from falsehood. I commit to examining the Scriptures daily, not out of suspicion, but out of love for the truth. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "When you hear a sermon or read a book, do you check its claims against Scripture? What holds you back?",
            actionSteps: ["After the next sermon or podcast you hear, look up the referenced Scriptures and read them in context", "Start a practice of daily Bible reading if you haven't already—even five minutes counts"]
        ),
        // Entry 18
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Nehemiah 8:8",
            scriptureText: "They read from the Book of the Law of God, making it clear, giving the meaning so that the people could understand what was being read.",
            devotionalTitle: "Understanding What You Read",
            devotionalText: "After decades of exile, Israel gathered to hear God's Word read aloud. But Ezra and the Levites didn't stop at reading—they explained it, clarified it, and helped the people grasp its meaning. God never intended His Word to be inaccessible. He provides teachers, study tools, and His own Spirit to help you understand. If you've ever felt lost reading the Bible, that's not a sign to stop—it's an invitation to seek help, ask questions, and dig deeper.",
            prayerText: "God, thank You for the gift of understanding. When I open Your Word and feel confused, remind me that You want me to comprehend what I read. Lead me to good teachers, faithful study resources, and fellow believers who can help me grasp Your truth. Send Your Holy Spirit to be my teacher as I read. Give me the humility to ask questions and the persistence to keep digging when a passage is difficult. I believe that You reward those who seek understanding. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What is one part of the Bible you find confusing, and what step could you take to understand it better?",
            actionSteps: ["Pick a difficult passage and use a study Bible or commentary to explore its meaning this week", "Ask your pastor or a mature believer to explain a verse you've always found puzzling"]
        ),
        // Entry 19
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Psalm 119:18",
            scriptureText: "Open my eyes that I may see wonderful things in your law.",
            devotionalTitle: "Open My Eyes",
            devotionalText: "This short, powerful prayer reveals something important: seeing the wonders of God's Word requires divine help. You can read the Bible with 20/20 vision and still miss its glory if the Spirit hasn't opened your eyes. The psalmist knew that true illumination comes from God Himself. Before you open the Bible, open your heart in prayer. Ask God to show you what you would otherwise overlook. The wonders are there—you just need eyes to see them.",
            prayerText: "Lord, open my eyes. I don't want to read Your Word with a closed heart or a distracted mind. Remove the veil that keeps me from seeing the wonders hidden in Scripture. Illuminate every page with the light of Your Spirit. Show me things I have never noticed before—truths that will transform the way I live. I come to Your Word today as a student and a seeker, asking You to be my teacher. Let me see the wonderful things You have placed in Your law. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Do you typically pray before reading the Bible? How might that practice change your experience of Scripture?",
            actionSteps: ["Begin every Bible reading this week with the simple prayer: 'Open my eyes, Lord'", "After reading, write down one 'wonderful thing' you noticed that you hadn't seen before"]
        ),
        // Entry 20
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "James 1:22-25",
            scriptureText: "Do not merely listen to the word, and so deceive yourselves. Do what it says. Anyone who listens to the word but does not do what it says is like someone who looks at his face in a mirror and, after looking at himself, goes away and immediately forgets what he looks like. But whoever looks intently into the perfect law that gives freedom, and continues in it—not forgetting what they have heard, but doing it—they will be blessed in what they do.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Mirror of the Word",
            devotionalText: "James compares Scripture to a mirror that shows you who you really are. But the power isn't in the glance—it's in the response. A person who reads the Bible and walks away unchanged is like someone who checks a mirror, notices food on their face, and does nothing about it. The blessing comes when you look intently, continue in the Word, and let it shape your actions. Scripture is meant to transform, not merely inform. Let it do its full work in you today.",
            prayerText: "Father, I don't want to be a forgetful hearer of Your Word. Help me to look intently into the perfect law that gives freedom and to continue in it. When Scripture reveals something that needs to change in my life, give me the courage to act on it immediately. I want the blessing that comes from being a doer, not just a listener. Guard me from self-deception, and let Your mirror show me the truth about who I am and who You are calling me to become. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What has God's Word recently shown you about yourself that you have not yet acted on?",
            actionSteps: ["After reading Scripture today, write down one specific action it calls you to take—and do it before the day ends", "Ask a trusted friend to hold you accountable to one thing God is showing you through His Word"]
        ),
    ]
}
